WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:02.675
Why does fund data need a standard?

2
00:00:02.725 --> 00:00:09.987
In this film you see how fund data travels, what goes wrong without a common format, and how FundsXML answers that.

3
00:00:10.037 --> 00:00:11.894
No knowledge of XML is needed.

4
00:00:13.144 --> 00:00:16.581
Behind every investment fund, data travels a long way.

5
00:00:16.631 --> 00:00:21.488
The asset manager, the fund administrator and the custodian bank produce it.

6
00:00:21.538 --> 00:00:33.369
Distributors and fund platforms, data vendors, insurance companies and regulators need it: prices and net asset values, portfolios, costs, and a growing number of regulatory templates.

7
00:00:33.419 --> 00:00:37.179
Every one of these parties exchanges data with many others.

8
00:00:38.529 --> 00:00:40.590
Take a single fund at month-end.

9
00:00:40.640 --> 00:00:45.339
The fund administrator calculates the net asset value and the positions.

10
00:00:45.389 --> 00:00:49.560
The asset manager checks the figures and adds its own fund data.

11
00:00:49.610 --> 00:00:54.178
Prices, costs and ESG data then go to fund platforms and distributors.

12
00:00:54.228 --> 00:00:59.191
Insurance companies that hold the fund need the full portfolio look-through.

13
00:00:59.241 --> 00:01:03.940
And data vendors and regulators receive master data, prices and reports.

14
00:01:03.990 --> 00:01:08.491
One fund, one date, and the same data travels to five places or more.

15
00:01:09.841 --> 00:01:16.655
Without a common format, every pair of partners agrees on its own files, with their own field names and codes.

16
00:01:16.705 --> 00:01:21.334
A new partner means a new interface, with mapping and testing from scratch.

17
00:01:21.384 --> 00:01:26.575
When regulation changes, every one of these interfaces has to be changed on its own.

18
00:01:26.625 --> 00:01:31.879
And errors surface late, often only at the receiver, when the data is already in use.

19
00:01:33.229 --> 00:01:35.003
What goes wrong in practice?

20
00:01:35.053 --> 00:01:36.241
First, identifiers.

21
00:01:36.291 --> 00:01:43.863
One partner identifies a fund by its ISIN, the next one by a national code, and the third by its own internal number.

22
00:01:43.913 --> 00:01:44.906
Second, meaning.

23
00:01:44.956 --> 00:01:54.613
The same word can mean different things: is a price the net asset value, the issue price, or the redemption price, and is a cost figure gross or net?

24
00:01:54.663 --> 00:01:56.046
Third, versions drift.

25
00:01:56.096 --> 00:02:02.496
Each partner changes its format on its own schedule, and every change breaks someone else's import.

26
00:02:02.546 --> 00:02:04.125
And finally, manual work.

27
00:02:04.175 --> 00:02:13.311
Spreadsheets and e-mails fill the gaps, people re-type numbers, and errors slip through, often unnoticed until a client or a supervisor asks.

28
00:02:14.661 --> 00:02:15.830
These costs add up.

29
00:02:15.880 --> 00:02:21.411
Each partner relationship needs its own mapping, its own tests and its own maintenance.

30
00:02:21.461 --> 00:02:25.452
Data is re-typed and re-formatted by hand, and checked by hand.

31
00:02:25.502 --> 00:02:30.712
Errors reach investors, insurers and regulators, and every correction costs trust.

32
00:02:30.762 --> 00:02:36.036
And every new requirement takes months, because it has to be built many times over.

33
00:02:37.386 --> 00:02:40.919
Over the last decade, regulation has become the main driver.

34
00:02:40.969 --> 00:02:46.352
MiFID II asks distributors to know the costs and the target market of every fund they sell.

35
00:02:46.402 --> 00:02:50.831
PRIIPs needs risk, cost and performance data for key information documents.

36
00:02:50.881 --> 00:02:54.891
The SFDR sends sustainability data from the fund to the distributor.

37
00:02:54.941 --> 00:02:59.728
And Solvency II asks insurers to look through to the real holdings of every fund.

38
00:02:59.778 --> 00:03:04.684
All of this is structured data, it changes regularly, and it has to arrive on time.

39
00:03:06.034 --> 00:03:12.974
For these needs, the industry body FinDatEx defines templates: which fields must be exchanged, and what they mean.

40
00:03:13.024 --> 00:03:17.328
But a template alone does not say how the data travels between systems.

41
00:03:17.378 --> 00:03:24.196
That is where FundsXML comes in: it carries these templates, together with all other fund data, in one delivery.

42
00:03:25.546 --> 00:03:29.407
FundsXML is the answer: one common language for fund data.

43
00:03:29.457 --> 00:03:34.801
Instead of many bilateral formats, every party writes and reads the same format.

44
00:03:34.851 --> 00:03:40.330
A sender produces a file once, and every receiver understands it, in the same way.

45
00:03:41.680 --> 00:03:43.183
The arithmetic is simple.

46
00:03:43.233 --> 00:03:49.021
Ten partners who all exchange data with each other need up to forty five bilateral interfaces.

47
00:03:49.071 --> 00:03:53.617
With a common format, each partner builds one: to read and write FundsXML.

48
00:03:53.667 --> 00:03:58.772
And a new partner no longer means a new project, because it speaks the same format.

49
00:04:00.122 --> 00:04:01.283
So what is FundsXML?

50
00:04:01.333 --> 00:04:08.247
It is open and free: the schema and its documentation are published, and anyone can use them without a licence fee.

51
00:04:08.297 --> 00:04:10.669
It was made by the fund industry itself.

52
00:04:10.719 --> 00:04:17.814
It started in two thousand and one, when fund houses looked for a common format for the data they exchanged every day.

53
00:04:17.864 --> 00:04:26.534
It is run by a not-for-profit association with open membership, and decisions are prepared in working groups by the people who use the standard.

54
00:04:26.584 --> 00:04:32.408
And it is built on XML, so every system can read it, and every file can be checked automatically.

55
00:04:33.758 --> 00:04:35.039
In short, its history.

56
00:04:35.089 --> 00:04:38.792
It began in two thousand and one, with a group of fund houses.

57
00:04:38.842 --> 00:04:44.359
In two thousand and three, version one was published under the German fund association, BVI.

58
00:04:44.409 --> 00:04:49.806
Over the following years, Austria, the Netherlands, France, Denmark and Luxembourg joined.

59
00:04:49.856 --> 00:04:57.129
Today, version four carries the European regulatory templates, and the standard keeps growing with every new requirement.

60
00:04:58.479 --> 00:05:01.533
Where does FundsXML fit among other standards?

61
00:05:01.583 --> 00:05:06.257
ISO 20022 covers messages, for example for fund orders and settlement.

62
00:05:06.307 --> 00:05:11.116
openfunds is a dictionary of fund attributes, often exchanged as tables.

63
00:05:11.166 --> 00:05:13.950
FinDatEx defines the regulatory templates.

64
00:05:14.000 --> 00:05:23.871
FundsXML brings it together in complete fund deliveries: master data, prices, portfolios, transactions and regulatory data, in one structured file.

65
00:05:23.921 --> 00:05:28.122
These standards work alongside each other; they do not compete.

66
00:05:29.472 --> 00:05:31.051
What does each party gain?

67
00:05:31.101 --> 00:05:35.939
Senders produce one format for all their partners, instead of one per partner.

68
00:05:35.989 --> 00:05:39.386
Receivers load data from every fund house the same way.

69
00:05:39.436 --> 00:05:43.521
Everyone can check a file automatically, before the data moves on.

70
00:05:43.571 --> 00:05:50.916
And when regulation changes, the new requirement is built into the standard once, and not into every single interface.

71
00:05:50.966 --> 00:05:54.989
In short: less effort for everyone, and data that can be trusted.

72
00:05:56.339 --> 00:05:59.134
This is the key idea: one file, many receivers.

73
00:05:59.184 --> 00:06:03.794
The same delivery can serve the distributor, the insurer and the data vendor.

74
00:06:03.844 --> 00:06:09.242
Each one takes the part it needs, and nobody has to build a special file for each partner.

75
00:06:10.592 --> 00:06:13.296
That was why fund data needs a standard.

76
00:06:13.346 --> 00:06:17.428
In the next film, you see what a FundsXML delivery contains.

77
00:06:17.478 --> 00:06:18.805
Thanks for watching.
